// C primitive: `@narrows(to: Kept)` on a parameter that takes a closure of
// one argument returning Bool, where Kept is one of its function's own type
// parameters, as in
//
//   function filter<Kept>(@narrows(to: Kept) let where: (Element) -> Bool): Array<Kept>
//
// At each call, Kept is what a true result of the closure written there
// narrows the closure's parameter to: an Apple for `{ fruit in fruit = Apple }`,
// the String for `{ name in name = .present }`, through `&` chains. It is the
// closure parameter's own type when the closure narrows nothing, when the
// two sides of `|` narrow it differently, or when the argument is not a
// closure written there, such as a parameter passed on.
//
// Inside the function, a call of the parameter in a condition, as in
// `switch { where(element): … }`, narrows its argument, a stable value, to
// Kept for the rest of the `&` chain and the arm, as a type test does, so
// the body is checked once against Kept. At run nothing changes: the
// argument is read as Kept holds it, the Apple out of its @fruit, the
// String out of its present String?.
@builtin
macro narrows(let to: TypeParameter): Parameter
